{
 "note": "Test 5: the constant-POWER ISA sweep, redone on pack-rebuilt floorplans at 34 cores where die areas span 4.7x (62-291 mm^2). Holding total watts fixed isolates COMPACTION, which is what the 27 August prediction was actually about. 42 points, scripts/isa_constant_power_pack.sh.",
 "CORRECTION_to_a_recorded_result": {
  "recorded_27_Aug": "isa_constant_power.json: '24 points attempted, 12 converged. Both compact dies diverge at BOTH power levels, including at 25 W where their densities are only 0.517 and 0.648 W/mm^2, comfortably under the grease cliff.'",
  "now": "ALL 42 POINTS CONVERGE, including arm_n1 at 0.965 W/mm^2 and 83.0 C.",
  "why": "the rebuilt floorplans are driven with --power-follows-area, which holds each block's power DENSITY at its baseline value. The old run kept McPAT's per-block POWER on re-sized blocks, so shrinking a die concentrated power into blocks whose sizes had changed but whose watts had not -- the same class of defect as the core_other slab and the results-broadcast-bus block. The 'half the sweep has no steady state' finding was therefore substantially an artefact of the power model, not a property of compact dies. The PHYSICAL point it was making -- that a compact die is penalised twice, by density and by a worse spreading boundary -- survives and is visible below; the divergence does not.",
  "what_still_stands": "the transferable lesson from that entry is untouched: any ISA comparison holding watts fixed must scale the cooler with die area, or it measures the package rather than the architecture."
 },
