  "peak_orders_monotonically_with_area": "smaller die runs hotter at the same watts: at 60 W, 62 mm^2 -> 83.0 C and 291 mm^2 -> 55.8 C, a 27 K spread from geometry alone",
  "AND_THE_COST_ORDERING_IS_INVERTED": "the compact die is HOTTER but CHEAPER to cool: at 60 W and 3 K margin, arm_n1 (62 mm^2, 83.0 C) costs 0.483 W while x86_golden_cove (291 mm^2, 55.8 C) costs 0.666 W. That is the OPPOSITE of the constant-DENSITY result, where compact dies were the most expensive. The two sweeps bracket the answer and they disagree because they hold different things fixed: at constant density a small die is cool and flat, so there is no concentrated peak to clip; at constant power it is hot and concentrated, so there is.",
  "cost_falls_with_die_power": "cooling gets cheaper as the die gets hotter -- 0.72-1.20 W at 25 W against 0.48-0.67 W at 60 W for the same 3 K -- because a hotter die has a steeper local gradient to work against."
